Market Challenges
Crystal Growth and Defect Control
Producing high-quality CZT crystals with consistent composition and low defect density remains technically demanding. Crystal imperfections can affect charge transport and detector resolution, while variations in wafer quality can reduce manufacturing yields. Improving growth processes and achieving repeatable material characteristics are essential for broader commercial adoption.

Limited Large-Scale Manufacturing Capacity
CZT production requires specialized equipment and experienced technical teams. Scaling manufacturing while maintaining material uniformity and reliable output can be difficult. These constraints may limit the availability of wafers for large-volume detector programs and create longer procurement timelines for customers.

Market Restraints
High Material and Processing Costs
Specialized crystal growth, wafer slicing, polishing, and quality inspection contribute to the cost of CZT wafers. These expenses can make CZT-based detectors less attractive in price-sensitive applications, particularly where alternative semiconductor materials provide adequate performance at lower cost.

Environmental and Regulatory Considerations
Cadmium is a toxic heavy metal, creating additional requirements for material handling, manufacturing waste management, and disposal. Compliance with environmental regulations can increase operating costs and complicate supply-chain management, particularly in regions with strict hazardous-material controls.

